once-example-clock
owner: fable-5.1 session 53f400ad
started: 2026-09-16
what: the operator's point — "calling feed twice is just inefficient;
  find a case where calling twice is RIGHT because the thing can have
  changed". It is the clock. The by-need example becomes a page
  handler that stamps its own duration: `val started = Fetch.now`
  (pinned, once), `def now = Fetch.now` (fresh at every mention, since
  time moves), `lazy val feed` (costly, and its two reads must see ONE
  list). Each word is now required for CORRECTNESS: `def` on started
  or `lazy val` on now would answer 0ms, `def` on feed could report
  one list's size with another's head. Plus the test harness the
  operator asked for: Reader carries your own data in, Writer carries
  the calls out, State carries a clock that moves because each call
  costs time.
files: src/test/scala/TestDirectOnce.scala, docs/direct-style.md, CHANGELOG.md
  also: a mark on a program of a NARROWER row — `!Reader.ask[Db]` in a
  block at `Writer % String + Reader % Db + State % Long` — is accepted
  and coerced through RowLift's In witness, which is what makes the
  harness read as ordinary code instead of `.plus[...]` per operation.
